Homestead Debian package
========================

This package provides the native Homestead daemon, homesteadctl, embedded
dashboard, curated catalog, systemd units, and catalog build assets. Debian 13
(trixie) is the package baseline. The amd64 binaries have run on the Debian 13
review appliance, but this `.deb` has not been installed there. The arm64
package is cross-built and inspected, but has not been run on arm64 hardware.
Ubuntu compatibility is not verified for this package build.

The package does not install Docker, Compose, cloudflared, Go, Node.js, or
application containers. Docker Engine with Compose v2 is needed to install
and run catalog apps. Public access additionally needs cloudflared and an
owner-configured tunnel. The source checkout bootstrap remains available for
preparing those host prerequisites.

Install with `sudo apt install ./homestead_<version>_amd64.deb` or the arm64
variant. The package starts and enables homestead-server. It creates the
Homestead state directories and system groups. It does not edit Docker's
daemon.json, restart Docker, claim the appliance, configure sign-in, or set up
public exposure.

Package upgrades replace package-owned binaries, catalog files, and units,
then restart the daemon. They preserve /etc/homestead, /var/lib/homestead,
/opt/homestead/stacks, Docker volumes, and mounted application data. The
active tunnel connector is not restarted by a package upgrade.

Removing or purging the package stops and disables the Homestead daemon and
tunnel service and removes package-owned files. It leaves appliance
configuration, SQLite state, Compose stacks, Docker containers and volumes,
mounted data, and the Homestead system groups in place. Containers and data
remain; they can still be managed with their Compose files. Access through
Homestead's tunnel depends on that service's state. Purge does not factory-reset
the appliance; use the separate, explicit factory-reset procedure only when
data deletion is intended.
